    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite passes: **66 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag so it returns `true` when an owner's city already holds between 40 and 49 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50), while leaving the existing hard rejection at 50 unchanged.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added a `CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40` constant. In the create flow I now compute the city's owner count once, keep the unchanged hard rejection (`>= 50` throws `CityCapacityException`), and set `capacityWarning` true when the count is in the 40\u201349 range (`>= 40 && < 50`), false otherwise.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with its getter/setter, mirroring the existing `bulkSignupWarning` warning field.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean property to the Owner schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it (MapStruct auto-maps it by name to the JSON response).\n\n4. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 Added a `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column so the persisted value survives the read-back (tests run against hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`).\n\nThe new `Cp54Tests` acceptance test (fill a fresh city to 40, then the next owner warns) passes, and all 66 existing tests remain green.",
